# Next.js Patterns

## Routing Model

- Use the App Router only.
- Follow Next.js file conventions such as `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `route.ts`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, and `not-found.tsx`.
- Do not introduce Pages Router patterns such as `pages/`, `getServerSideProps`, or `getStaticProps`.

## Server And Client Components

- Prefer Server Components by default.
- Add `"use client"` only when the file needs hooks, event handlers, browser APIs, or client-only libraries.
- Keep server-only logic out of Client Components.

## Auth And Layouts

- Follow the authenticated layout pattern already used in `@app/app/layout.tsx`.
- Server layouts may call `createSessionClient()` and redirect unauthenticated users to `/login`.
- Prefer route protection in server layouts and server code instead of relying on client redirects alone.

## Data Fetching

- For interactive app screens, prefer calling internal API routes and consuming them through SWR hooks.
- Reuse patterns from `@lib/use-data.ts` before creating a new client data layer.
- Keep fetchers simple and colocate reusable hooks in `lib/` or `hooks/` when they serve multiple screens.

## Navigation And Assets

- Use `next/link` for internal navigation.
- Use Next.js-native primitives when appropriate.
- Keep route segments and folders aligned with existing app structure.

## Prefer

- Server layout auth checks
- SWR hooks for interactive pages
- Client pages only when interactivity is required

## Avoid

- Adding `"use client"` to every page by default
- Fetching Supabase directly from many unrelated Client Components when an API route already fits better
- Introducing old Next.js router APIs

## Reference Files

- `@app/app/layout.tsx`
- `@app/page.tsx`
- `@app/login/page.tsx`
- `@lib/use-data.ts`
